**Return summary (≤250 words):**

(a) What was written: A 12-item, 5-phase implementation spec for bathos v0.6 with verified file:line anchors, fixer-decomposable atoms (3–5 per item), verification gates, and risk tables. Items span bathos (Python) and praxia (Rust). Phase 2 is the foundational diff: exception-swallowing remediation + pre-execution manifest + `outcome="error"` in a single coherent change to runner.py/sidecar.py/prereg.py/compact.py/schema.py. Maraxiom §3 capabilities (`bth cite`, `bth lineage --format prov`) fold into bathos as items 7 and 8.

(b) Three anchor drifts from the recon brief: (1) Exception swallow count is ~10–12 sites, not 7 — runner.py has additional config-read swallows at lines 117–118, 137–143, 146–152, and query.py:440–441 not listed in the brief. The spec categorizes all by severity. (2) The `outcome="error"` bug is at `runner.py:225–232` as a block; the fix point is inserting a guard before line 225, not modifying line 232 itself (the brief called line 232 "THE BUG"). (3) `linter.py:82–127` is Tier-1 naming hooks only; Tier-2 hooks are at lines 137–280; the adversarial_check hook is a new function added alongside them, not inserted into 82–127.

(c) Three issues for oracle: (1) `evaluate_outcome` raise semantics — callers need auditing before atom-1a; confirm callers catch SidecarError and map to `outcome="error"` (not propagate to CLI). (2) Item 11 scope risk — if sprint-composer doesn't exist, Phase 5 becomes a design sprint; oracle should confirm acceptable scope risk threshold. (3) Schema v5 atomicity — if items 2 and 6 land in separate PRs, one bump or two?
